=== Q001
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: What does a bank reconciliation compare?
+ The cash at bank ledger account (cash book) with the bank statement || Explains differences.
- Receivables with payables || Not a bank reconciliation.
- Sales with purchases || Not related.
- The trial balance with the SOFP || Not related.
explain: It helps detect errors, fraud and unrecorded items.
mistake: Thinking the bank statement is always right.
tip: Cash book vs bank.
src: Study text ch13 - purpose (concept basis; original question)

=== Q002
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Which item requires an adjustment to the CASH BOOK?
+ Bank charges shown only on the bank statement || Unrecorded item.
- An unpresented cheque || Bank statement side.
- An outstanding deposit || Bank statement side.
- An error made by the bank || Bank statement side.
explain: Unrecorded items and cash book errors are corrected in the ledger.
mistake: Adjusting the cash book for timing differences.
tip: Unknown to us = update cash book.
src: Study text ch13 - differences (concept basis; original question)

=== Q003
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: How are unpresented cheques treated in a bank reconciliation?
+ Deducted from the bank statement balance || Not yet cleared by the bank.
- Added to the bank statement balance || That is for outstanding deposits.
- Deducted from the cash book || Already recorded there.
- Ignored || They are reconciling items.
explain: The bank will deduct them when presented.
mistake: Adjusting the cash book.
tip: Unpresented = deduct from bank.
src: Study text ch13 - unpresented cheques (concept basis; original question)

=== Q004
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: Which cash figure is shown in the statement of financial position?
+ The corrected (revised) cash book balance || After adjustments.
- The bank statement balance || Includes timing differences.
- The average of the two || Not correct.
- The opening balance || Not correct.
explain: Timing differences do not change the true position.
mistake: Using the bank statement balance.
tip: Revised cash book.
src: Study text ch13 - SOFP figure (concept basis; original question)

=== Q005
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: How are outstanding deposits (lodgements) treated in a bank reconciliation?
+ Added to the bank statement balance || Not yet credited by the bank.
- Deducted from the bank statement balance || That is for unpresented cheques.
- Added to the cash book || Already recorded.
- Ignored || Reconciling items.
explain: The bank will credit them shortly.
mistake: Adjusting the cash book.
tip: Lodged but not cleared = add to bank.
src: Study text ch13 - deposits (concept basis; original question)

=== Q006
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: A customer's cheque is returned unpaid by the bank. How is this recorded in the cash book?
+ Debit receivables; credit cash at bank || Reverse the receipt.
- Debit cash at bank; credit receivables || That records a receipt.
- No entry - it is a timing difference || The cash book must be updated.
- Debit irrecoverable debts; credit bank || Not yet irrecoverable.
explain: The customer owes the money again.
mistake: Treating it as a timing difference.
tip: Dishonoured = reverse.
src: Study text ch13 - dishonoured cheques (concept basis; original question)

=== Q007
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: On a bank statement, what does a CREDIT balance usually mean?
+ The account has money in it - the bank owes the customer || Mirror image of the cash book.
- The account is overdrawn || That is a debit on a statement.
- The bank has charged interest || Not necessarily.
- An error has occurred || Not necessarily.
explain: In the cash book, money in the bank is a debit.
mistake: Reading the statement as if it were the cash book.
tip: Bank's view is reversed.
src: Study text ch13 - statement balances (concept basis; original question)

=== Q008
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t4
status: review
q: What is a supplier statement reconciliation?
+ A comparison of the supplier's statement with the business's payables ledger account for that supplier || Explains differences.
- A comparison of cash book and bank statement || Bank reconciliation.
- A list of receivables || Aged receivables.
- A trial balance || Not related.
explain: Payments in transit are a common difference.
mistake: Confusing with bank reconciliations.
tip: Supplier statement vs our ledger.
src: Study text ch13 - supplier statements (concept basis; original question)

=== Q009
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: A direct debit appears on the bank statement but has not been recorded in the cash book. What should happen?
+ Record it in the cash book as a payment || Unrecorded item.
- Add it to the bank statement balance || Not a timing difference.
- Ignore it || It must be recorded.
- Treat it as an outstanding deposit || Not a deposit.
explain: Standing orders are treated the same way.
mistake: Treating it as a reconciling item.
tip: Bank knows, we don't = update cash book.
src: Study text ch13 - direct debits (concept basis; original question)

=== Q010
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: Which is a purpose of a bank reconciliation?
+ To detect errors and possible fraud || A key control.
- To calculate depreciation || Not related.
- To value inventory || Not related.
- To record sales tax || Not related.
explain: It also confirms the cash figure for the SOFP.
mistake: Seeing it as unnecessary paperwork.
tip: Control over cash.
src: Study text ch13 - purpose (concept basis; original question)

=== Q011
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
scenario: The cash book shows a debit balance of $5,200. The bank statement shows bank charges of $45, a direct credit from a customer of $600 and a direct debit of $300, none yet in the cash book.
q: What is the revised cash book balance ($)?
answer: 5455
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Revised = cash book + receipts not recorded - payments not recorded
step: 5,200 + 600 = 5,800.
step: 5,800 - 45 - 300 = $5,455.
check: 5200+600-45-300
explain: This is the SOFP cash figure.
mistake: Ignoring bank charges.
tip: Update the cash book first.
src: Study text ch13 - revised balance (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q012
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
scenario: The bank statement shows a credit balance of $6,100. Unpresented cheques total $1,400 and an outstanding deposit is $755.
q: What is the balance ($) per the reconciliation, which should agree with the revised cash book?
answer: 5455
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Bank statement - unpresented cheques + outstanding deposits
step: 6,100 - 1,400 = 4,700.
step: 4,700 + 755 = $5,455.
check: 6100-1400+755
explain: This agrees with the revised cash book.
mistake: Adding unpresented cheques.
tip: Cheques out; deposits in.
src: Study text ch13 - reconciliation (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q013
type: mr
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
select: 2
q: Which TWO items are reconciling items on the BANK STATEMENT side?
+ Cheques written but not yet presented to the bank || Timing difference.
+ An error made by the bank || Bank's error.
- Bank interest charged || Cash book item.
- A standing order paid by the bank || Cash book item.
explain: Unrecorded items and cash book errors go in the cash book.
mistake: Mixing sides.
tip: Timing and bank errors = bank side.
src: Study text ch13 - two types (concept basis; original question)

=== Q014
type: match
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Match each item with where it is adjusted.
pair: Bank charges => Cash book
pair: Unpresented cheque => Bank statement balance (deduct)
pair: Outstanding lodgement => Bank statement balance (add)
pair: Cash book transposition error => Cash book
explain: The cash book is corrected; the statement is reconciled.
mistake: Correcting the cash book for timing differences.
tip: Our errors = our book.
src: Study text ch13 - classification (concept basis; original question)

=== Q015
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
q: A customer receipt of $540 was recorded in the cash book as $450. By how much ($) must the cash book balance be increased?
answer: 90
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Correction = correct amount - recorded amount
step: 540 - 450.
step: = $90 increase.
check: 540-450
explain: Transposition errors are divisible by 9.
mistake: Adding 540 again.
tip: Correct the difference only.
src: Study text ch13 - errors (concept basis; original question)

=== Q016
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: What are the two main stages of preparing a bank reconciliation?
+ Update the cash book, then reconcile the bank statement balance to the revised cash book || Standard approach.
- Reconcile first, then ignore the cash book || Cash book must be updated.
- Change the bank statement figures || Statements are not changed.
- Prepare the trial balance first || Not needed.
explain: Both stages should arrive at the same figure.
mistake: Skipping the cash book update.
tip: Update, then reconcile.
src: Study text ch13 - format (concept basis; original question)

=== Q017
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
scenario: The cash book shows an overdraft of $2,000. Bank charges of $80 have not been recorded.
q: What is the revised cash book overdraft ($)?
answer: 2080
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Revised overdraft = overdraft + unrecorded charges
step: Overdraft = -2,000.
step: -2,000 - 80 = -2,080 (overdraft of $2,080).
check: 2000+80
explain: Charges make the overdraft larger.
mistake: Reducing the overdraft.
tip: Treat overdrafts as negatives.
src: Study text ch13 - overdrafts (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q018
type: mcq
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
scenario: The bank statement shows an overdraft of $1,800. Unpresented cheques are $900 and an outstanding lodgement is $620.
q: What balance should the revised cash book show?
+ Overdraft of $2,080 || -1,800 - 900 + 620.
- Overdraft of $1,520 || Signs reversed for cheques.
- Credit of $280 || Not correct.
- Overdraft of $3,320 || Adds lodgement wrongly.
formula: Statement balance - unpresented + outstanding lodgements
step: -1,800 - 900 = -2,700.
step: -2,700 + 620 = -2,080: overdraft $2,080.
check: -1800-900+620
explain: Agrees with the revised cash book overdraft.
mistake: Treating the overdraft as positive.
tip: Use negatives.
src: Study text ch13 - overdraft reconciliation (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q019
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t4
status: review
q: A supplier's statement shows a payment the business sent on the last day of the month as not yet received. What type of difference is this?
+ A payment in transit (timing difference) || Will clear shortly.
- An error by the business || Not an error.
- An unrecorded invoice || Not an invoice.
- A disputed item || Not disputed.
explain: No adjustment to the ledger is needed.
mistake: Adjusting the ledger.
tip: In transit = timing.
src: Study text ch13 - supplier differences (concept basis; original question)

=== Q020
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Interest received from the bank appears on the bank statement only. How is it treated?
+ Debit cash at bank; credit interest received in the cash book || Unrecorded receipt.
- Deduct it from the bank statement balance || Not a timing difference.
- Add it to the bank statement balance || Not correct.
- Ignore it || Must be recorded.
explain: Direct credits are treated the same way.
mistake: Treating it as a reconciling item.
tip: Update the cash book.
src: Study text ch13 - interest (concept basis; original question)

=== Q021
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t4
status: review
scenario: A supplier's statement shows $8,400 owed. The business's ledger shows $6,900. A payment of $1,200 is in transit and an invoice of $300 has not yet been recorded by the business.
q: After recording the invoice, what will the business's ledger balance be ($)?
answer: 7200
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Revised ledger = ledger + unrecorded invoice
step: 6,900 + 300 = 7,200.
step: Statement 8,400 - payment in transit 1,200 = 7,200 - they agree.
check: 6900+300
explain: The payment in transit is a timing difference.
mistake: Adjusting the ledger for the payment.
tip: Record omissions, reconcile timing.
src: Study text ch13 - supplier reconciliation (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q022
type: mcq
level: S
cat: trap
topic: t3
status: review
q: A trainee deducts unpresented cheques from the CASH BOOK balance. What is wrong?
+ The cheques are already recorded in the cash book; they are deducted from the bank statement balance || Double counting.
- Nothing is wrong || Double counts.
- They should be added to the cash book || Not correct.
- They should be ignored || They are reconciling items.
explain: Cash would be understated twice.
mistake: Adjusting the wrong side.
tip: Already in our book.
src: Study text ch13 - unpresented cheques (concept basis; original question)

=== Q023
type: order
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: Put the bank reconciliation steps in order.
item: Compare the cash book with the bank statement and tick matching items
item: Update the cash book for unrecorded items and errors
item: Calculate the revised cash book balance
item: Reconcile the bank statement balance for timing differences and bank errors
explain: The two figures should then agree.
mistake: Reconciling before updating.
tip: Tick, update, revise, reconcile.
src: Study text ch13 - steps (concept basis; original question)

=== Q024
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: The bank has wrongly charged another customer's cheque to the business's account. How is this treated?
+ As a reconciling item on the bank statement side, and the bank is asked to correct it || Bank error.
- Recorded in the cash book as a payment || Not the business's payment.
- Ignored || Must be reconciled.
- Written off as an expense || Not an expense.
explain: The cash book is not changed for bank errors.
mistake: Recording the bank's error.
tip: Bank error = bank side.
src: Study text ch13 - bank errors (concept basis; original question)

=== Q025
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
q: The cash book shows a debit balance of $3,600. A customer's cheque for $350 has been dishonoured, and a standing order of $120 has not been recorded. What is the revised cash book balance ($)?
answer: 3130
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Revised = balance - dishonoured cheque - standing order
step: 3,600 - 350 = 3,250.
step: 3,250 - 120 = $3,130.
check: 3600-350-120
explain: Both items reduce cash.
mistake: Adding the dishonoured cheque.
tip: Bounced = money out.
src: Study text ch13 - updates (concept basis; original question)

=== Q026
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t4
status: review
q: Why might a business send aged statements to customers?
+ To remind them of amounts owed and any overdue balances || Credit control.
- To pay suppliers || Not related.
- To calculate depreciation || Not related.
- To record sales tax || Not related.
explain: Customers can query differences early.
mistake: Thinking statements are only for suppliers.
tip: Remind and chase.
src: Study text ch13 - customer statements (concept basis; original question)

=== Q027
type: mcq
level: S
cat: trap
topic: t1
status: review
q: A trainee uses the bank statement balance as the cash figure in the SOFP "because the bank is always right". What is wrong?
+ The bank statement excludes timing differences and may contain errors; the revised cash book is used || Correct figure.
- Nothing is wrong || Not correct.
- The SOFP should show zero cash || Not correct.
- The opening cash figure should be used || Not correct.
explain: Unpresented cheques have already been paid out by the business.
mistake: Using the statement figure.
tip: Revised cash book.
src: Study text ch13 - SOFP (concept basis; original question)

=== Q028
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
q: The revised cash book balance is $7,800 (debit). Unpresented cheques total $2,300 and outstanding deposits $1,100. What should the bank statement show ($)?
answer: 9000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Bank statement = revised cash book + unpresented cheques - outstanding deposits
step: 7,800 + 2,300 = 10,100.
step: 10,100 - 1,100 = $9,000 (credit).
check: 7800+2300-1100
explain: Work backwards from the cash book to the statement.
mistake: Reversing the adjustments.
tip: Reverse the formula.
src: Study text ch13 - working back (concept basis; original question)

=== Q029
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Which item is a TIMING difference?
+ A cheque paid to a supplier on the last day of the year that the bank has not yet processed || Will clear later.
- Bank charges not in the cash book || Unrecorded item.
- A transposition error in the cash book || Cash book error.
- A dishonoured cheque || Cash book adjustment.
explain: Timing differences resolve themselves in the next period.
mistake: Confusing with unrecorded items.
tip: Just not cleared yet.
src: Study text ch13 - timing (concept basis; original question)

=== Q030
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t4
status: review
q: An invoice appears on the supplier's statement but not in the business's ledger. What should the business do?
+ Check the invoice is valid and, if so, record it in the payables ledger || Unrecorded invoice.
- Ignore it until the next statement || Should be followed up.
- Deduct it from the statement balance || Only if a timing difference.
- Pay it immediately without checking || Should be verified.
explain: Disputed items should be raised with the supplier.
mistake: Paying without checking.
tip: Verify then record.
src: Study text ch13 - supplier reconciliation (concept basis; original question)

=== Q031
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
scenario: Cash book debit balance $12,450. Bank statement items not in the cash book: bank charges $65; direct credit $1,320; direct debit $410. A cheque payment of $285 was entered in the cash book as $258.
q: What is the revised cash book balance ($)?
answer: 13268
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Revised = balance + credits - charges - direct debits - error correction
step: 12,450 + 1,320 - 65 - 410 = 13,295.
step: Error: payment understated by 27; 13,295 - 27 = $13,268.
check: 12450+1320-65-410-(285-258)
explain: The payment was 27 more than recorded, reducing cash.
mistake: Correcting the error the wrong way.
tip: Payment understated = cash too high.
src: Study text ch13 - revised balance (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q032
type: mcq
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
scenario: Revised cash book balance $13,268 debit. Unpresented cheques $3,940. Outstanding lodgement $2,150. The bank wrongly debited a $500 cheque of another customer to the account.
q: What balance should the bank statement show?
+ $14,558 credit || 13,268 + 3,940 - 2,150 - 500.
- $15,058 credit || Ignores the bank error.
- $11,978 credit || Signs reversed.
- $13,268 credit || No reconciling items applied.
formula: Statement = revised cash book + unpresented - lodgements - bank's wrong debit
step: 13,268 + 3,940 - 2,150 = 15,058.
step: The bank wrongly took out 500, so the statement shows 14,558.
check: 13268+3940-2150-500
explain: The bank error lowers the statement balance.
mistake: Ignoring the bank error.
tip: Think about what the bank did.
src: Study text ch13 - full reconciliation (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q033
type: mr
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
select: 2
q: Which TWO items should be entered in the cash book when preparing a reconciliation?
+ A standing order for insurance paid by the bank || Unrecorded payment.
+ A BACS receipt from a customer shown on the statement || Unrecorded receipt.
- An outstanding lodgement || Timing difference.
- A cheque not yet presented || Timing difference.
explain: Timing differences are only reconciling items.
mistake: Recording timing differences.
tip: Only what we didn't know.
src: Study text ch13 - cash book updates (concept basis; original question)

=== Q034
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
scenario: The bank statement shows an overdraft of $4,600. Unpresented cheques are $2,100 and outstanding lodgements $3,400.
q: What is the correct cash balance ($)? (Enter a negative number for an overdraft.)
answer: -3300
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Correct = statement balance - unpresented + lodgements
step: -4,600 - 2,100 = -6,700.
step: -6,700 + 3,400 = -3,300 (overdraft).
check: -4600-2100+3400
explain: The overdraft is a current liability in the SOFP.
mistake: Treating the overdraft as positive.
tip: Signs carefully.
src: Study text ch13 - overdraft (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q035
type: mcq
level: C
cat: trap
topic: t2
status: review
q: A trainee records an outstanding lodgement as a receipt in the cash book again. What is the effect?
+ Cash is overstated, because the deposit was already recorded || Double counting.
- No effect || Double counted.
- Cash is understated || Overstated.
- Receivables are overstated || Receivables are reduced too much.
explain: Outstanding lodgements are only reconciling items.
mistake: Double recording.
tip: Already in the book.
src: Study text ch13 - lodgements (concept basis; original question)

=== Q036
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t4
status: review
scenario: Supplier statement balance $15,750. Ledger balance $12,180. Differences: payment of $2,500 in transit; credit note of $430 not yet received by the business (on the statement); invoice of $1,500 on the statement not recorded by the business.
q: After the business records the missing items, what is its revised ledger balance ($)?
answer: 13250
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Revised ledger = ledger + unrecorded invoice - unrecorded credit note
step: 12,180 + 1,500 - 430 = 13,250.
step: Statement 15,750 - payment in transit 2,500 = 13,250 - agreed.
check: 12180+1500-430
explain: The payment in transit is a timing difference only.
mistake: Adjusting the ledger for the payment.
tip: Record omissions; reconcile timing.
src: Study text ch13 - supplier reconciliation (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q037
type: mcq
level: C
cat: scenario
topic: t1
status: review
q: A cashier prepares the bank reconciliation and also writes and records all cheques. Why is this a control weakness?
+ The same person could hide fraud in the reconciliation; duties should be segregated || Lack of segregation.
- Reconciliations are not needed || They are needed.
- The bank prepares reconciliations || The business does.
- Cashiers are not allowed to see bank statements || Not the issue.
explain: An independent person should prepare or review it.
mistake: Ignoring segregation of duties.
tip: Different people, different jobs.
src: Study text ch13 - controls (concept basis; original scenario)

=== Q038
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
scenario: The cash book balance is $8,920 debit. The receipts column was overcast by $200. Bank charges of $55 and interest received of $38 appear only on the statement.
q: What is the revised cash book balance ($)?
answer: 8703
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Revised = balance - overcast - charges + interest
step: 8,920 - 200 = 8,720.
step: 8,720 - 55 + 38 = $8,703.
check: 8920-200-55+38
explain: Overcasting receipts overstated cash.
mistake: Adding the overcast.
tip: Overcast receipts = reduce cash.
src: Study text ch13 - errors (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q039
type: mcq
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: Why can't unpresented cheques be ignored in a reconciliation?
+ They explain why the bank statement balance is higher than the cash book || Timing difference.
- Because they are cash book errors || They are not errors.
- Because they are bank errors || Not bank errors.
- Because they are income || Not income.
explain: Without them, the balances will not agree.
mistake: Ignoring them.
tip: They explain the gap.
src: Study text ch13 - unpresented cheques (concept basis; original question)

=== Q040
type: match
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t4
status: review
q: Match each supplier statement difference with its treatment.
pair: Payment in transit => Timing difference - no ledger adjustment
pair: Invoice not recorded by the business => Record it in the ledger if valid
pair: Disputed invoice => Raise with the supplier
pair: Credit note issued by supplier not yet recorded => Record it in the ledger
explain: Only genuine omissions or errors change the ledger.
mistake: Adjusting for timing differences.
tip: Timing vs omission vs dispute.
src: Study text ch13 - supplier differences (concept basis; original question)

=== Q041
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
scenario: Bank statement credit balance $9,780. Unpresented cheques: #301 $1,250; #302 $890; #305 $2,140. Outstanding lodgement $3,600.
q: What is the balance ($) per the reconciliation?
answer: 9100
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Statement - unpresented cheques + outstanding lodgements
step: Unpresented total = 1,250 + 890 + 2,140 = 4,280.
step: 9,780 - 4,280 + 3,600 = $9,100.
check: 9780-(1250+890+2140)+3600
explain: This should equal the revised cash book.
mistake: Missing one cheque.
tip: Total the cheques first.
src: Study text ch13 - reconciliation (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q042
type: mcq
level: C
cat: trap
topic: t3
status: review
q: The cash book shows a debit balance of $500 and the bank statement shows "$500 DR". A trainee says they agree. What is wrong?
+ On a bank statement, DR means overdrawn, so the two balances are opposite || Mirror image.
- Nothing is wrong || They are opposite.
- Both show an overdraft || Cash book debit = money in the bank.
- Both show money in the bank || Statement shows overdrawn.
explain: Read statement balances from the bank's point of view.
mistake: Reading DR the same way.
tip: Bank's debit = your overdraft.
src: Study text ch13 - mirror image (concept basis; original scenario)

=== Q043
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
q: A cash book was undercast by $1,000 on the payments side, and a receipt of $360 was recorded twice. The original balance was $6,740 debit. What is the revised balance ($)?
answer: 5380
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Revised = balance - payments undercast - duplicated receipt
step: 6,740 - 1,000 = 5,740.
step: 5,740 - 360 = $5,380.
check: 6740-1000-360
explain: Both errors overstated cash.
mistake: Adding the undercast.
tip: Payments undercast = cash too high.
src: Study text ch13 - errors (concept basis; original question)

=== Q044
type: mcq
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: How often should bank reconciliations ideally be prepared?
+ Regularly, such as monthly, so errors and fraud are found quickly || Timely control.
- Only at the year end || Too infrequent.
- Never, if the bank is reliable || Always needed.
- Only when an auditor asks || Too late.
explain: Frequent reconciliations keep differences small.
mistake: Leaving it to the year end.
tip: Little and often.
src: Study text ch13 - frequency (concept basis; original question)

=== Q045
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
scenario: Revised cash book: overdraft $1,250. Unpresented cheques $3,800. Outstanding lodgements $1,950.
q: What balance ($) should the bank statement show? (Enter a negative number if overdrawn.)
answer: 600
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Statement = revised cash book + unpresented - lodgements
step: -1,250 + 3,800 = 2,550.
step: 2,550 - 1,950 = $600 in credit.
check: -1250+3800-1950
explain: The bank shows money in hand because cheques have not cleared.
mistake: Keeping the overdraft sign throughout.
tip: Reverse the reconciliation.
src: Study text ch13 - working back (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q046
type: mcq
level: C
cat: scenario
topic: t2
status: review
q: A large unpresented cheque from four months ago is still not cleared. What should be considered?
+ Whether the cheque has been lost, cancelled or is stale, and whether to reverse it in the cash book || Investigate old items.
- Nothing - it is a normal timing difference || Too old.
- Deduct it twice || Not correct.
- Record it as income || Not income.
explain: Old reconciling items may hide errors.
mistake: Leaving old items unexamined.
tip: Old timing differences = investigate.
src: Study text ch13 - stale items (concept basis; original scenario)

=== Q047
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
scenario: Cash book debit $3,870. Unrecorded: bank charges $90; dishonoured customer cheque $420; direct credit $1,150. Unpresented cheques $2,060; outstanding lodgement $780.
q: What does the bank statement show ($)?
answer: 5790
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Revised cash book, then statement = revised + unpresented - lodgements
step: Revised = 3,870 - 90 - 420 + 1,150 = 4,510.
step: Statement = 4,510 + 2,060 - 780 = $5,790.
check: 3870-90-420+1150+2060-780
explain: Both stages are needed.
mistake: Applying timing differences to the cash book.
tip: Two stages.
src: Study text ch13 - full reconciliation (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q048
type: mcq
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t4
status: review
q: What is the MAIN benefit of reconciling supplier statements regularly?
+ Errors, missing invoices and disputes are identified before payment || Accurate payables.
- It removes the need to pay suppliers || Not true.
- It increases purchases || Not related.
- It replaces the bank reconciliation || Different control.
explain: It also protects supplier relationships.
mistake: Paying statements without checking.
tip: Check before paying.
src: Study text ch13 - benefits (concept basis; original question)

=== Q049
type: mcq
level: F
cat: revision
topic: t2
status: review
important: 1
q: Which statement about bank reconciliations is correct?
+ Unrecorded items and cash book errors adjust the cash book; timing differences and bank errors adjust the bank statement balance || Core rule.
- All differences adjust the cash book || Timing differences do not.
- All differences adjust the bank statement || Unrecorded items do not.
- The bank statement balance goes in the SOFP || Revised cash book.
explain: Both sides should then agree.
mistake: Mixing up sides.
tip: Ours vs theirs.
src: Study text ch13 - summary (concept basis; original question)

=== Q050
type: mcq
level: S
cat: revision
topic: t3
status: review
important: 1
q: Which reconciliation formula is correct?
+ Bank statement - unpresented cheques + outstanding deposits ± bank errors = revised cash book || Core formula.
- Bank statement + unpresented cheques - outstanding deposits = cash book || Signs reversed.
- Cash book + unpresented cheques = bank statement - deposits || Partly reversed.
- Cash book = bank statement always || Not true.
explain: Treat overdrafts as negative numbers.
mistake: Reversing signs.
tip: Cheques out, deposits in.
src: Study text ch13 - formula (concept basis; original question)
